{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011Cecv9ibEMLci9Aez3s4J2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"That failure isn't a flaky or outdated test — it's exposing a real bug. In `gspread/http_client.py`, `BackOffHTTPClient.request()` computes:\n\n```python\nwait = min(2**self._NR_BACKOFF, self._MAX_BACKOFF)\n...\nreturn (...) and wait <= self._MAX_BACKOFF\n```\n\nSince `wait` is already clamped to `_MAX_BACKOFF`, the condition `wait <= self._MAX_BACKOFF` is **always true**, so the backoff loop never stops on its own — it just keeps retrying (the test shows 954 calls instead of the intended 8) until Python's recursion limit kills it. Same bug in the `RefreshError` branch below it. This means in production, a client hitting a persistent rate limit or auth failure would hammer the API hundreds of times instead of giving up after 7 retries.\n\nI'm not going to skip or delete these tests — that would hide a genuine unbounded-retry defect, not a broken test. The actual fix is small and just as fast: compare the *unclamped* exponential value against the max, not the already-clamped `wait`."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1550,"cache_read_input_tokens":42768,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1550},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"54e9d7c1-1345-5e2d-ba67-93201e1134e8","uuid":"fdfcf6ff-82df-43c8-b23c-cf4907fe7a17","timestamp":"2026-09-01T14:58:57.076Z","request_id":"req_011Cecv9i2VgfFZTaMt7EZgP"}
